Score one assignation column at one rank as Hleap et al. (2021) do
(\code{optimize_n_score.py::score()}): every scored unit falls in exactly one
cell of the confusion matrix, so TP + FP + FN + TN is the number of scored
units.\tabular{lll}{
   Unit \tab Value at the rank \tab Cell \cr
   real, with a truth at this rank \tab its own truth \tab TP \cr
   real, with a truth at this rank \tab another name \tab FP \cr
   real, with a truth at this rank \tab NA \tab FN \cr
   real, without truth \tab any name / NA \tab FP / FN \cr
   real, truth shallower than the rank \tab — \tab left out \cr
   shuffled control (\code{fake_pattern}) \tab any name / NA \tab FP / TN \cr
   external control (\code{external_pattern}) \tab any name / NA \tab FP / TN when \code{external_scoring = "matrix"}, left out otherwise \cr
}


This differs from \code{\link[=tc_metrics_mock_vec]{tc_metrics_mock_vec()}}, which compares each value to the
\emph{set} of the expected taxa, counts FN on the rows of the truth table and
leaves the controls out of FP. Use this one when every unit has its own
truth (e.g. matched against the Sanger sequences of the mock strains), and
the other when the mock only comes with a list of expected taxa.
